it's a good idea to keep these executables under 4k, because micronix
loads at 0x1000.

in a pinch, the boot code could put all buffers except the indirect
block into a non-loaded BSS above 4k; that will gain 512 bytes.

this directory contains the standalone and cp/m boot programs
for the djdma, hddma and ide controllers.

a boot happens in three levels.  level 0 is the rom's own step, and on
three of the four ways in it is nothing this directory builds: the
monitor reads a whole 512 byte sector to 0100 and jumps to it, so the
rom is level 0 and the sector it read is level 1.  level 1 is that
sector - it moves itself out of the way, reads the second level to
8000, unpacks the object header and jumps to its text.  level 2 is the
filesystem code, boot.c, which finds the kernel on the disk and loads
it.

the DJ-DMA is the exception, because its rom cannot be level 0.  that
controller has its own z80, running a channel program out of the host's
memory, and all the firmware leaves behind is the first 128 bytes of
the first sector, copied to 0080.  that is not enough to hold a level 1,
so the floppy's first stage builds one: djboot1.s leads with a 128 byte
level 0 whose whole job is to read the next block and jump into it, and
the level 1 behind it is mwboot1.s with a channel program for a read.

the second level is one source - boot.c - and three drivers, because
what differs is how a block is read: mwio.c for the HD-DMA, djio.c for
the floppy, ideio.c for the IDE card.  so there are three second
levels, and each belongs to the first level that loads it:

	mwboot.com	the hard disk		loaded by mwboot1.s
	djboot.com	the floppy		loaded by djboot1.s
	ideboot.com	the ide card		loaded by ideboot1.s

all three are linked at 0100, which is both where cp/m loads a .com and
where the monitor's hard disk boot enters, so one binary serves either
way in.

mwboot1.s is the first level for the monitor's way in.  the rom reads
one sector, 512 bytes, to 0100 and jumps to it; mwboot.com is several
sectors, so something has to fetch the rest, and that sector is this.
it moves itself to c000 first, because what it is loading is linked at
0100 and would otherwise land on the loop doing the loading.

it sets up almost nothing.  the rom used the channel at 0080 to read the
first level in, so the constants are loaded, the drive is selected and
the heads are at cylinder 0 - only the sector, the dma address and the
opcode change.  65 bytes of the 512.

ideboot1.s is the same sector for the IDE card, except that the card
has no command block in memory to be handed a sector with: this one
writes the drive's task file and moves the sector through the 8255 by
hand.  it has to fit in the half of the sector ahead of the label, so
it is written shorter than the other two.

the floppy image is laid out around the same label, which is why its
first level cannot all be in block 0:

	block 0		level 0, its first 128 bytes
	block 1		level 1, which reads blocks 2 through 9
	blocks 2-9	the second level, djboot.com
	blocks 10-39	unused; the filesystem starts at block 40

mkfs -i puts mwboot1/mwboot in a file that owns cylinder 0, block 0
being the first level and block 1 onward the second.  the second half
of block 0 holds the disk label (sys/dlabel.h), which is why the first
level has to fit in the first half - and why the floppy's level 1, 206
bytes, cannot share block 0 with level 0 and the label both, and has a
block of its own instead.
